Topic

Python Object-Oriented Programming (OOP)

What I Learned

Today I focused on several core concepts of Python’s Object-Oriented Programming model.

Class vs Instance Variables

* Class variables are defined directly inside the class and are shared by every object created from that class.
* Instance variables are created inside the __init__ method using self and belong to each individual object.

The __init__ Constructor

* The __init__ method runs automatically whenever a new object is created.
* It is used to initialise an object’s starting attributes.
* It can also call other methods within the class using self.

Calling Methods with self

* When one method needs to call another method within the same class, it should use self.method_name().
* Using self ensures the method is called on the current object instance.

__str__ vs __repr__

* __str__ provides a readable, user-friendly representation of an object, typically used with print().
* __repr__ provides a more detailed representation intended for debugging and development.

Key Takeaway

Today’s lesson reinforced my mental model of object-oriented programming:

* A class is the blueprint.
* An object is an instance created from that blueprint.
* self refers to the current object.
* __init__ is responsible for setting up each object when it is created.

Building a strong understanding of these fundamentals will make it easier to write and understand object-oriented Python code as I continue learning.
